From 0bc9a8b0570d597808223ff8ed2a3c1f5bcc2e52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:18:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ipv4: send IP ID 0 for connected non-TCP atomic datagrams

ip_select_ident_segs() gives every packet from a connected socket an ID
from the per-socket inet_id, and for non-TCP sockets, which can send
without the socket lock, that is an atomic_add_return() since commit
f866fbc842de ("ipv4: fix data-races around inet->inet_id"). For a
datagram with DF set and skb->ignore_df clear -- an atomic datagram in
RFC 6864 terms, from a socket using IP_PMTUDISC_DO or IP_PMTUDISC_PROBE
-- no reassembly uses the ID, and the unconnected path of the same
function already sends 0.

Send 0 for such datagrams from connected non-TCP sockets as well,
skipping the atomic. TCP is unchanged. Sockets using the default
IP_PMTUDISC_WANT are unchanged, because __ip_make_skb() sets
skb->ignore_df for them.

This changes the IP ID on the wire for connected UDP, SCTP, raw and
ping sockets that use IP_PMTUDISC_DO/PROBE, from a per-socket counter
to 0.

Not measured. Build-tested only.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
DO NOT SEND. Recommendation: DROP. Kept for the record; see REVIEW.md.

Impact: low -- connected UDP/SCTP/raw/ping sockets that set
IP_PMTUDISC_DO or IP_PMTUDISC_PROBE and are shared by several sending
threads; benefit not measured.

Why dropped:
- Wire-visible (IP ID counter -> 0) for a saving that was never
  measured. The existing harness section could not reach this code:
  each process opens its own socket, and the default IP_PMTUDISC_WANT
  sets skb->ignore_df, so the new branch is never taken.
- UDP GSO: with a base ID of 0, every GSO send produces IDs 0..n-1 and
  the next send restarts at 0, so receive-side UDP GRO (inet_gro_flush()
  accepts only incrementing or fixed IDs) cannot merge across two sends.
  Today the per-socket counter keeps the sequence continuous.
- Also reaches SCTP (sctp sets inet_daddr), raw and ping sockets, not
  only UDP as the original changelog said.

History checked (git log -L on ip_select_ident_segs()):
- pre-2.6.12: connected DF packets use inet->id "to work around buggy
  Windows95/2000 VJ compression implementations"; unconnected DF get 0.
- 703133de331a (2013) "ip: generate unique IP identificator if local
  fragmentation is allowed": the !skb->ignore_df test.
- 23f57406b82d (2022) "ipv4: avoid using shared IP generator for
  connected sockets": all connected packets use the private generator,
  citing "Off-Path TCP Exploits of the Mixed IPID Assignment".
- 431280eebed9 (2018), 970a5a3ea86d (2022): TCP RST/ACK/SYNACK sent
  with ID 0 to close off-path side channels.
- f866fbc842de (2023) "ipv4: fix data-races around inet->inet_id": the
  atomic for non-TCP.
Upstream has moved toward 0 for DF control packets and never chose the
counter specifically for connected UDP, so this does not reverse a
recorded decision; it is dropped on cost/benefit, not on history.

 include/net/ip.h | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/net/ip.h b/include/net/ip.h
index 6f602df72ee6..26dbe7bbf4dc 100644
--- a/include/net/ip.h
+++ b/include/net/ip.h
@@ -597,6 +597,13 @@ static inline void ip_select_ident_segs(struct net *net, struct sk_buff *skb,
 			sock_owned_by_me(sk);
 			val = atomic_read(&inet_sk(sk)->inet_id);
 			atomic_set(&inet_sk(sk)->inet_id, val + segs);
+		} else if ((iph->frag_off & htons(IP_DF)) && !skb->ignore_df) {
+			/* Atomic datagram (RFC 6864): no reassembly uses the ID;
+			 * the unconnected case below already sends 0. Avoids
+			 * the atomic on a socket shared by lockless senders.
+			 */
+			iph->id = 0;
+			return;
 		} else {
 			val = atomic_add_return(segs, &inet_sk(sk)->inet_id);
 		}

